/* Reading comprehension skills: CZ8 main idea and title, CZ9 fact and detail, CZ10 inference,
   CZ11 vocabulary in context, CZ12 tone and the author's attitude.
   RCP is a pool of short original passages. Each question carries its passage in its own text, so it stands alone;
   one passage is asked about in several lessons, each time with a different question. */
const RCP = {
  P1: "In many Indian towns, rain that falls on rooftops simply runs into drains and is lost. A simple pit or tank can capture this water and send it back into the ground. Over a few years, such pits can raise the level of water in nearby wells. Several housing societies that tried the idea now buy far fewer water tankers in summer. The method is cheap, needs little upkeep and can be copied almost anywhere.",
  P2: "Children today spend hours each day in front of screens. Doctors warn that too much screen time, especially late at night, can disturb sleep and strain the eyes. Yet screens are not harmful in themselves; a well-chosen video can teach a child more about the planets than a dull textbook. The real task for parents is not to ban devices but to set sensible limits and choose good content.",
  P3: "The idea of zero as a number, and not merely an empty place, was developed by Indian mathematicians more than a thousand years ago. Brahmagupta, writing in the seventh century, gave rules for adding and subtracting with zero. The idea travelled west through Arab scholars and slowly changed how people everywhere did arithmetic. Without it, much of modern mathematics and computing would be hard to imagine.",
  P4: "Every year, crores of plastic bags are used for a few minutes and then thrown away. They choke drains, harm cattle that swallow them and break into tiny pieces that remain in the soil for centuries. Bans have been announced many times, but in many markets they are quietly ignored. Unless shoppers carry their own bags and shopkeepers stop offering free plastic, rules on paper will change little.",
  P5: "In the past few years, paying through a mobile phone has become common even at small tea stalls in India. A customer scans a code, enters a PIN, and the money moves within seconds. For shopkeepers, this means less need to keep change and fewer trips to the bank. However, the ease of these payments has also attracted fraudsters, who trick people into sharing their PINs. Users therefore need to be as careful with their phones as they are with their wallets.",
  P6: "A brisk walk of thirty minutes a day costs nothing, yet its benefits are remarkable. Regular walkers tend to have healthier hearts, better control over their weight and a calmer mind. Unlike many sports, walking needs no special skill, partner or equipment. It is perhaps the easiest habit a busy person can build for a healthier life.",
  P7: "For centuries, many Indian households were joint families, with grandparents, parents and children living under one roof. As people moved to cities for work, smaller nuclear families became more common. The change has given young couples more privacy and freedom of choice. At the same time, many elderly people now live alone, missing the daily company and care that the old system offered.",
  P8: "India receives plenty of sunshine for most of the year, and the cost of solar panels has fallen sharply over the last decade. As a result, solar power is now among the cheapest sources of new electricity in the country. Farmers are using solar pumps to water their fields, and many villages that once had no power now light their homes with the sun. If storage batteries become cheaper too, the sun could meet a much larger share of India’s needs.",
  P9: "A false message on a phone can now reach lakhs of people before anyone checks whether it is true. Some such rumours have caused panic, and a few have even led to violence. The people who forward them are often not wicked; they simply believe that they are warning their friends. A moment’s pause to check the source of a message could prevent a great deal of harm.",
  P10: "Millets such as jowar, bajra and ragi were once everyday food in large parts of India. They grow well on dry land, need far less water than rice and are rich in fibre and minerals. Over time, many families gave them up for polished rice and wheat, which seemed more modern. Today, with water growing scarce and lifestyle diseases on the rise, these humble grains are winning back their place on the plate.",
  P11: "During the pandemic, schools moved their classes online almost overnight. In cities, many students could follow lessons on laptops or phones. In villages, however, a single phone was often shared by three or four children, and the network was weak. The experience showed that technology can widen the gap between the rich and the poor unless access to it is made fair.",
  P12: "A public library is one of the few places where anyone, rich or poor, can sit for hours without being asked to buy anything. It offers books, newspapers and a quiet corner to students who have no space to study at home. Many successful people recall that their love of learning began in such a room. Yet in many towns, libraries are short of funds, and their shelves hold books that are decades old.",
  P13: "On winter mornings, a grey haze hangs over many north Indian cities. Smoke from vehicles, dust from building sites and the burning of crop waste all add to it. People with weak lungs, children and the elderly suffer the most. Officials often respond with short-term steps such as closing schools for a few days, but the haze returns every year because its causes are left untouched.",
  P14: "In 2023, India’s Chandrayaan-3 landed near the south pole of the Moon, a region no country had landed in before. The mission cost far less than many space projects abroad, showing what careful planning can achieve on a modest budget. Its rover moved across the surface and sent back data about the soil. For millions of young Indians watching the landing live, it was a moment of great pride.",
  P15: "In many villages, weavers still make cloth on hand looms, using designs passed down for generations. Each saree can take weeks to finish. Machine-made copies, however, sell at a fraction of the price, and many young people from weaving families are leaving the craft for jobs in towns. Unless buyers are willing to pay a fair price for handmade work, these skills may be lost within a generation.",
  P16: "Computer programs can now study X-ray images and point out signs of disease, sometimes as accurately as trained doctors. In crowded hospitals, such tools could help doctors see more patients in less time. Experts, however, insist that the final decision must stay with a human, because a program can make mistakes it cannot explain. Used as an assistant rather than a replacement, the technology may save many lives.",
  P17: "Our town proudly opened a new ‘smart’ parking system last year. Drivers now download an app, create an account, verify their phone number, choose a slot and pay online, all to park for ten minutes outside the vegetable market. Those without smartphones simply park on the road as before. Truly, technology has made our lives simpler.",
  P18: "Before the printing press, every book had to be copied by hand, so books were rare and costly. When Gutenberg began printing with movable metal type in Europe around 1450, books could be produced quickly and in large numbers. Ideas now spread faster than ever, and more people learned to read. Many historians see printing as one of the inventions that shaped the modern world.",
  P19: "Mangrove forests grow along muddy coasts where rivers meet the sea. Their tangled roots hold the soil together and slow down storm waves before they reach villages. They also shelter young fish, which later support the fishing families of the coast. Despite this, large stretches of mangroves have been cleared for fish farms and buildings, leaving the coast more open to storms.",
  P20: "When floods struck a district last year, college students from nearby towns arrived within days. They packed food, sorted clothes and helped families clean their homes. None of them was paid, and many missed weeks of classes. Their work was a reminder that the spirit of service is alive among the young, even when it rarely makes the news."
};
/* RQ(lesson, passage, question, [right, wrong, wrong, wrong], explanation) */
const RQ = (l, p, ask, o, e) => QB.push([l, "Read the passage: “" + RCP[p] + "” " + ask, o, e, ""]);

LESSONS.push(
{id:"CZ8", heat:2, title:"RC: the main idea and the title", hi:"अपठित गद्यांश: मुख्य विचार और शीर्षक", blocks:[
  {k:"hot", h:"Almost every SSC passage has one question on the <b>main idea</b>, the <b>central theme</b> or the <b>most suitable title</b>. The right option covers the <b>whole</b> passage; the wrong ones are a single detail, something far too wide, or a twist of what was said."},
  {k:"story", h:"<p>Imagine telling a friend in one breath what a passage was about. That sentence is its <b>main idea</b>. A <b>title</b> is the same idea squeezed into a few words.</p><p>The main idea is usually stated or shaped in the <b>first</b> sentence and rounded off in the <b>last</b>. The middle gives reasons and examples. So read the first and last lines again, and ask: which option would every sentence of the passage agree to support?</p><p>Then test each option. Is it <b>too narrow</b> — only one example? <b>Too broad</b> — about the whole world when the passage is about one town? <b>Distorted</b> — the passage’s words, but a claim it never made? <b>Extreme</b> — <i>always</i>, <i>never</i>, <i>all</i>? What survives is the answer.</p>", key:"The main idea is the one claim every sentence of the passage supports — not too narrow, not too broad."},
  {k:"table", title:"Testing a main-idea option", head:["The option is…","What it looks like","Verdict"], rows:[
    ["Too narrow","One example or one sentence of the passage","Wrong — it is a detail"],
    ["Too broad","A claim about everything (all cities, the whole world)","Wrong — the passage is more limited"],
    ["Distorted","Uses the passage’s words but changes the claim","Wrong — check it against the lines"],
    ["Extreme","Always, never, all, only, completely","Usually wrong"],
    ["Outside","True in life but not discussed in the passage","Wrong"],
    ["Just right","Covers the beginning, the middle and the end","Right"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Where the main idea hides", head:["Passage shape","Where to look","Example"], rows:[
    ["Problem → solution","The solution, often in the last line","‘Unless shoppers carry their own bags, rules will change little.’"],
    ["Old belief → new view","The new view","‘Zero is a number, not merely an empty place.’"],
    ["Gains and losses","Both sides together","‘The change has given privacy … at the same time, the elderly live alone.’"],
    ["Praise of one thing","The thing and why it matters","‘A brisk walk … its benefits are remarkable.’"]]},
  {k:"ex", h:"<p><b>Worked RC passage.</b> ‘Bees do much more than make honey. As they move from flower to flower, they carry pollen that many crops need in order to produce fruit and seed. Farmers who grow mustard, apples or sunflowers depend on them more than they realise. When bee numbers fall because of pesticides, harvests can fall too.’</p><p><b>Main idea?</b> (a) Bees make honey — <i>too narrow, and the passage says ‘much more than’</i>. (b) Pesticides are harmful to all life — <i>too broad</i>. (c) Without bees every crop dies — <i>extreme</i>. (d) <b>Bees matter to farming because they carry pollen that crops need</b> — covers all four sentences. <b>Best title:</b> ‘Bees: The Farmer’s Hidden Helpers’.</p>"},
  {k:"trick", h:"<b>“First line + last line = main idea.”</b> Then use the <b>umbrella test</b>: the right option is an umbrella under which every sentence of the passage can stand."},
  {k:"trap", h:"A title that repeats a striking word from the passage is <b>not</b> automatically right. ‘Water Tankers’ appears in a passage on rainwater harvesting, but it is only a detail. And a title should name the <b>topic</b>, not a <b>moral</b> the passage never draws."}
], sum:["The main idea is the one claim every sentence supports; a title says it in a few words.","Look at the first and last lines first.","Reject options that are too narrow (one detail), too broad, distorted, extreme or outside the passage.","For a ‘gains and losses’ passage, the main idea mentions both sides.","A striking word from the passage does not make an option right."]},

{id:"CZ9", heat:3, title:"RC: fact and detail questions", hi:"अपठित गद्यांश: तथ्य और विवरण के प्रश्न", blocks:[
  {k:"hot", h:"Two of the five RC questions are usually <b>fact questions</b> — ‘According to the passage…’, ‘Which is NOT mentioned…’, ‘Why did…’. They are the surest marks in the section, because the answer is written in the passage, though usually in <b>different words</b>."},
  {k:"story", h:"<p>A fact question is a treasure hunt with a map. The <b>key word</b> of the question — a name, a number, an unusual word — is the map. Find it in the passage, read the sentence it is in and the one around it, and the answer is there.</p><p>The examiner rarely copies the passage word for word into the right option. He <b>rephrases</b> it: ‘buy far fewer water tankers’ may become ‘need less water from outside’. Meanwhile a wrong option often copies the passage’s words exactly but gets the fact wrong. So match the <b>meaning</b>, not the words.</p><p>For a <b>NOT mentioned</b> question, turn it round: tick off each option you can find in the passage; the one left over is the answer.</p>", key:"Find the key word, read its sentence, match the meaning (not the exact words)."},
  {k:"table", title:"Fact question stems and the move to make", head:["Stem","Move"], rows:[
    ["According to the passage, …","Find the key word; read one sentence before and after"],
    ["Which of the following is true?","Check each option against the passage; one contradiction kills it"],
    ["Which is NOT mentioned / NOT true?","Tick off the options you find; the leftover is the answer"],
    ["Why did …?","Look for because, since, so, as a result near the key word"],
    ["What happened when / after …?","Follow the order of events in the passage"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"How wrong fact options are made", head:["Trick","Example from a passage on electric buses"], rows:[
    ["Opposite of the text","‘Fares are higher than on the old buses’ (the passage says they are the same)"],
    ["Right words, wrong link","‘The buses are charged at stops during the day’ (they are charged overnight at the depot)"],
    ["Not in the passage","‘The buses were made in India’ (never stated)"],
    ["Wrong number or name","‘Each bus can run 100 km on a charge’ (the passage says 200)"]]},
  {k:"ex", h:"<p><b>Worked RC passage.</b> ‘The city’s first electric buses began running in March. Each bus can travel about 200 kilometres on one charge and is charged overnight at the depot. The buses are quieter than diesel ones and give off no smoke. Fares are the same as on the old buses.’</p><p><b>Q.</b> According to the passage, when are the buses charged? Key word: <i>charged</i> → sentence 2 → <b>overnight, at the depot</b>. <b>Q.</b> Which is NOT mentioned? (a) the buses are quieter ✓ found; (b) they give off no smoke ✓ found; (c) fares are unchanged ✓ found; (d) <b>they were made in India</b> — not found, so this is the answer.</p>"},
  {k:"trick", h:"<b>“Key word → line → meaning.”</b> Never answer a fact question from memory of the passage; go back to the line every time — it takes ten seconds and saves two and a half marks."},
  {k:"trap", h:"An option that copies a whole phrase from the passage can still be wrong if it joins it to the <b>wrong subject or time</b>. And in a NOT question, an option that is <b>true in real life</b> but absent from the passage is the answer, not a wrong option."}
], sum:["Fact questions: According to the passage, which is true, which is NOT mentioned, why did.","Find the key word, read its sentence and the one around it.","Right options usually rephrase the passage; wrong ones often copy its words with a changed fact.","For NOT mentioned, tick off what you find; the leftover is the answer.","Watch for opposites, wrong links, wrong numbers and outside facts."]},

{id:"CZ10", heat:2, title:"RC: inference questions", hi:"अपठित गद्यांश: अनुमान (Inference) के प्रश्न", blocks:[
  {k:"hot", h:"‘It can be inferred…’, ‘The passage suggests…’, ‘The author would most likely agree…’. One RC question in many shifts asks for what the passage <b>implies</b> but does not say. The right answer is <b>one small, safe step</b> beyond the text — never a leap."},
  {k:"story", h:"<p>If a friend comes in with a wet umbrella, you infer that it is raining outside. You did not see the rain, but the umbrella makes it almost certain. That is an <b>inference</b>: a conclusion the facts force on you.</p><p>Notice what you did <i>not</i> infer: that it will rain all week, or that your friend hates rain. Those may be true, but the umbrella does not prove them.</p><p>Inference questions work the same way. The right option is something the passage makes <b>almost certain</b>. A wrong option either just repeats a stated fact (that is a fact question, not an inference), or goes much further than the passage allows.</p>", key:"An inference is one small step the passage forces — not a big leap it merely allows."},
  {k:"table", title:"Is it a sound inference?", head:["The option…","Verdict","Why"], rows:[
    ["Must be true if the passage is true","Right","This is what an inference is"],
    ["Could be true, but the passage does not show it","Wrong","A guess, not an inference"],
    ["Goes against something the passage says","Wrong","A contradiction"],
    ["Uses extreme words (all, never, only)","Usually wrong","Too big a step"],
    ["Brings in outside knowledge","Wrong","The passage must support it"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"Clues that point to an inference", head:["The passage says…","You may infer…"], rows:[
    ["‘X can raise the level of water in wells’","The level was lower before"],
    ["‘less need to keep change’","They needed to keep change before"],
    ["‘If batteries become cheaper too …’","Batteries are costly now"],
    ["‘not wicked; they simply believe …’","They mean well"],
    ["‘Truly, technology has made our lives simpler’ after describing a long process","The author means the opposite (irony)"]]},
  {k:"ex", h:"<p><b>Worked RC passage.</b> ‘When the new road reached the village, the weekly bus became a daily one. Within a year, two clinics and a bank branch opened near the bus stand. Girls who had left school after Class 8 began to travel to the high school in the town.’</p><p><b>What can be inferred?</b> (a) <b>Before the road, the village was harder to reach and had fewer services</b> — forced by the facts ✓. (b) Every girl in the village now goes to college — <i>extreme leap</i>. (c) The bank was built by the government — <i>outside the passage</i>. (d) The bus now runs daily — <i>stated, so not an inference</i>.</p>"},
  {k:"trick", h:"<b>The “must be true” test</b>: read each option and ask, ‘If everything in the passage is true, <i>must</i> this be true as well?’ Only one option passes."},
  {k:"trap", h:"An option that is stated in so many words is <b>not</b> an inference, though it is true — prefer the option that follows from the text. And do not choose a statement just because you agree with it."}
], sum:["An inference is what the passage makes almost certain without saying it.","The right option is one small step from the text; wrong ones are leaps, guesses or contradictions.","Use the must-be-true test on every option.","‘The author would most likely agree’ asks for a view in line with the author’s stated views.","Irony: when praise does not match the facts, the author means the opposite."]},

{id:"CZ11", heat:2, title:"RC: vocabulary in context", hi:"अपठित गद्यांश: प्रसंग में शब्दार्थ", blocks:[
  {k:"hot", h:"One or two RC questions ask the meaning (or the opposite) of a word ‘<b>as used in the passage</b>’. The trap is a <b>true dictionary meaning that does not fit this sentence</b> — <i>modest</i> as ‘humble in manner’ when the passage means ‘a modest budget’ (a small one)."},
  {k:"story", h:"<p>Many English words have several meanings. <i>Recall</i> can mean ‘remember’ or ‘call back a faulty product’. <i>Common</i> can mean ‘widespread’ or ‘vulgar’. <i>Struck</i> can mean ‘hit’ or ‘went on strike’. In a passage, only one meaning is alive.</p><p>So never answer a vocabulary question from the word alone. Go to its sentence and <b>replace the word with each option</b>. The right option keeps the sentence’s meaning; a wrong one makes it odd or changes it.</p><p>For an <b>antonym</b> question, first find the word’s meaning in the sentence, then pick its opposite in that same sense.</p>", key:"Put each option in place of the word; the one that keeps the sentence’s meaning is right."},
  {k:"table", title:"Words whose meaning changes with the sentence", head:["Word","In this sentence…","…it means"], rows:[
    ["modest","The mission was run on a modest budget.","small, limited"],
    ["modest","Despite his fame, he remained modest.","humble"],
    ["recall","Many people recall their first library visit.","remember"],
    ["recall","The company will recall the faulty phones.","call back"],
    ["common","Mobile payments have become common.","widespread"],
    ["common","The two villages share a common well.","shared"],
    ["struck","When floods struck the district …","hit suddenly"],
    ["struck","The workers struck for higher wages.","went on strike"],
    ["sharp","Prices showed a sharp rise.","sudden and large"],
    ["sharp","She gave him a sharp reply.","harsh"],
    ["sound","It is a sound plan.","sensible, reliable"],
    ["sound","The child slept a sound sleep.","deep"],
    ["table","They will table the proposal at the meeting.","present for discussion"],
    ["humble","These humble grains are back on the plate.","simple, ordinary"]]},
  {k:"table", title:"The four-step method", head:["Step","What you do"], rows:[
    ["1","Find the word in the passage and read its whole sentence"],
    ["2","Guess the meaning in your own words before looking at the options"],
    ["3","Replace the word with each option and read the sentence again"],
    ["4","Reject the option that is a real meaning of the word but not the one used here"]]},
  {k:"ex", h:"<p><b>Worked example.</b> ‘The mission cost far less than many space projects abroad, showing what careful planning can achieve on a <b>modest</b> budget.’</p><p>Options: <i>small / shy / humble in manner / decent in dress</i>. A budget cannot be shy or humble; those are meanings for people. Put <b>small</b> in: ‘on a small budget’ — the sentence keeps its sense. Answer: <b>small</b>.</p>"},
  {k:"trick", h:"<b>“Swap and read.”</b> Swap the word for each option and read the sentence aloud in your head. The right one sounds like the passage; the trap sounds like a dictionary."},
  {k:"trap", h:"The <b>most familiar</b> meaning is often the trap. And in an <b>antonym</b> question, one option is usually a <b>synonym</b> — read the question twice so you do not pick the same meaning when the opposite is asked."}
], sum:["Answer ‘as used in the passage’ — the meaning in that sentence, not the commonest one.","Guess the meaning first, then swap each option into the sentence.","Familiar dictionary meanings that do not fit are the usual traps (modest, recall, common, struck).","For an antonym, fix the sense first, then find its opposite; watch for a synonym among the options."]},

{id:"CZ12", heat:2, title:"RC: tone and the author’s attitude", hi:"अपठित गद्यांश: लेखक का भाव और दृष्टिकोण", blocks:[
  {k:"hot", h:"‘The tone of the passage is…’ or ‘The author’s attitude towards X is…’ comes up in many shifts. The options are tone words — <i>critical, appreciative, objective, ironic, nostalgic, optimistic</i> — so you need to know what each means and which <b>signal words</b> reveal it."},
  {k:"story", h:"<p>The same fact can be told in many voices. ‘The new road was finished in two years.’ — that is <b>objective</b>. ‘At last, after two long years of dust and delay, the road is finished!’ — that is <b>relieved</b>, even <b>critical</b> of the delay. ‘A mere two years for one kilometre — what speed!’ — that is <b>ironic</b>: the words praise, but the facts mock.</p><p>Tone lives in the author’s <b>choice of words</b>. Praise words (<i>remarkable</i>, <i>gift</i>, <i>pride</i>) give an appreciative tone. Blame words (<i>ignored</i>, <i>careless</i>, <i>fails</i>) give a critical one. Worry words (<i>harm</i>, <i>danger</i>, <i>may be lost</i>) give a concerned one. Plain facts with no such words give an objective one.</p><p>SSC passages are usually <b>moderate</b>. Extreme tones such as <i>furious</i>, <i>hysterical</i> or <i>despairing</i> are rarely right.</p>", key:"Tone is in the word choice: praise, blame, worry, hope, longing — or none (objective)."},
  {k:"table", title:"Tone words and what they mean", head:["Tone word","Meaning","Signal in the text"], rows:[
    ["objective","Without personal feeling","Facts and figures; no praise or blame"],
    ["critical","Finding fault","‘ignored’, ‘fails’, ‘left untouched’"],
    ["appreciative","Valuing, praising","‘remarkable’, ‘gift’, ‘pride’"],
    ["ironic","Saying the opposite of what is meant","Praise that the facts contradict"],
    ["sarcastic","Mocking, meant to wound","‘Oh, brilliant work!’ for a failure"],
    ["satirical","Using humour to attack foolishness","Exaggeration of a public folly"],
    ["nostalgic","Longing for the past","‘in those days’, ‘once upon a time’"],
    ["optimistic","Hopeful about the future","‘could’, ‘promising’, ‘may save many lives’"],
    ["pessimistic","Expecting the worst","‘nothing will change’, ‘doomed’"],
    ["didactic","Teaching a moral lesson","‘we must’, ‘one should always’"],
    ["cynical","Believing people act only for themselves","‘as usual’, ‘of course they did’"],
    ["sympathetic","Sharing others’ hardship","‘struggle’, ‘hardship’, ‘fair price’"],
    ["indifferent","Showing no interest","Flat, uncaring language"],
    ["laudatory","Full of high praise","‘heroic’, ‘outstanding’"],
    ["condescending","Looking down on others","‘simple folk who cannot understand’"],
    ["humorous","Meant to amuse","Jokes, light exaggeration"]]},
  {k:"ex", h:"<p><b>Worked RC passage.</b> ‘Our town proudly opened a new “smart” parking system last year. Drivers now download an app, create an account, verify their phone number, choose a slot and pay online, all to park for ten minutes outside the vegetable market. Truly, technology has made our lives simpler.’</p><p>The last line praises, but the long list of steps shows the opposite — parking became harder. When the words and the facts disagree, the tone is <b>ironic</b>. It is not <i>appreciative</i> (that reads the words, not the facts) and not <i>objective</i> (the author clearly has a view).</p>"},
  {k:"trick", h:"<b>“Count the feeling words.”</b> No feeling words → objective. Praise → appreciative. Blame → critical. Worry → concerned. Hope → optimistic. Praise that the facts mock → ironic."},
  {k:"trap", h:"<b>Ironic</b> and <b>sarcastic</b> are close: irony says the opposite of what is meant; sarcasm is irony meant to hurt someone. <b>Indifferent</b> (not caring) is not the same as <b>objective</b> (fair, without bias). Avoid extreme tones unless the passage is extreme."}
], sum:["Tone is shown by the author’s choice of words.","Objective = no feeling words; critical = blame; appreciative = praise; concerned = worry; optimistic = hope.","Ironic = the words say the opposite of what the facts show; sarcastic = irony meant to wound.","Nostalgic = longing for the past; didactic = teaching a lesson; cynical = doubting people’s motives.","SSC passages are usually moderate; extreme tones are rarely right.","Objective is not indifferent."]}
);

/* CZ8 — main idea and title */
RQ("CZ8","P1","What is the main idea of the passage?",["Collecting rooftop rainwater in pits is a cheap and effective way to save water","Towns in India should build more and bigger drains","Water tankers cost housing societies too much in summer","The wells of Indian towns have dried up completely"],"The passage describes the problem, the method, its results and its low cost — all about rooftop rainwater harvesting. The tankers are only one result.");
RQ("CZ8","P1","Which is the most suitable title for the passage?",["Saving the Rain from the Roof","The Problem of Blocked Drains","Why Water Tankers Are Costly","The Old Wells of Indian Towns"],"The whole passage is about capturing rooftop rain; the other titles pick out details.");
RQ("CZ8","P2","What is the main idea of the passage?",["Screens should be used with sensible limits and good content rather than banned","Screens are the main cause of poor sleep in children","Videos teach children better than any textbook can","Parents should take all devices away from their children"],"The last sentence gives the point: not a ban, but limits and good content. The passage says a good video beats a dull textbook — not every textbook.");
RQ("CZ8","P3","What is the main idea of the passage?",["The Indian idea of zero as a number changed mathematics across the world","Brahmagupta was the greatest mathematician of all time","Arab scholars invented modern arithmetic","Computers cannot work without Indian scholars"],"The passage traces zero from India through the Arab world to all of modern mathematics. The other options are extreme or distorted.");
RQ("CZ8","P3","Which is the most suitable title for the passage?",["Zero: India’s Gift to Arithmetic","The Life of Brahmagupta","Arab Scholars of the Middle Ages","How Computers Are Built"],"The passage is about the idea of zero and its spread; Brahmagupta and the Arab scholars are parts of that story.");
RQ("CZ8","P4","What is the main idea of the passage?",["Bans on plastic bags will work only if shoppers and shopkeepers change their habits","Plastic bags are useful for a few minutes","Cattle in Indian markets are often sick","No rule about plastic bags has ever been announced"],"The passage moves from the harm of plastic bags to failed bans and ends with the real solution: a change in habits.");
